---
title: Handles own nodes
summary: Why typed Q handles are the ownership roots of a model and ids are weak, and why collection is mark-and-sweep that runs only when reachability can shrink.
order: 5
---

## Context

Models grow and shrink at run time. A sliding-window filter adds and removes quantities every tick,
and marginalization replaces quantities with factors. Nodes that nothing uses must be reclaimed
without every mutation having to report what it orphaned.

## Decision

A `Q<B, T>` is the ownership token. `cleanup` is a mark-and-sweep whose roots are the ids that live
handles name plus the likelihood terms, and it follows parent edges, bindings, and scored-quantity
edges. A bare `Id` is a weak reference. Collection runs at the mutations that reduce reachability
(`remove`, `remove_closure`, `demote_to_parameter`), never on `add_*`.

## Consequences

- Reachability is recomputed, not reported, so no mutation can forget to report an orphan.
- Every node kind is collectable, and the sweep cannot fail.
- Building an $n$-node model stays $O(n)$; a grow-only workload keeps its garbage until it asks,
  and watches `graph_node_count`.
- An `Id` kept across `cleanup()` may name nothing (`NodeNotFound`).

## Alternatives considered

A candidate-queue collector preceded this one. It kept a queue of ids that *might* be garbage and
popped each one once, so a "not yet" verdict was final. Take `y = f(x)` and `z = g(y)` with both
handles dropped: examining `z` took it and made `y` a leaf, but `y` had already been popped and
stayed in the graph for good unless some later mutation offered it again. So every mutation that
reduced reachability had to hand back what it orphaned. That obligation was spread across five
call sites and enforced by nothing. The old rule also spared every `Constant` and `Parameter`, in
case a caller still held a bare `Id`, and so leaked every temporary constant.

Mark-and-sweep takes `z` and `y` in one pass, because neither is marked, and nothing has to report
anything: reachability is a property of the graph, not an event.
